're just really difficult to test  because we don't have a lot of good  experimental data on some of the bats  how likely is it that we will discover a  really powerful drug that here  coven 19 quickly before a vaccine gets  licensed for widespread use for most of  those drugs not all but most that are  being examined for treatments for coven  19 a lot of them are already in use for  other other infections or other areas so  if we could find something that is  effective and it has already been  licensed for something else it's already  known to be you know relatively safe and  has already gone through all the  approvals it's much easier to take those  and kind of repurpose them for treatment  for Coppa 19 than it is to develop  something completely de novo where you  have to you know determine its safety  profile and and everything else but from  you know the tests we've seen so far and  of course there are many that are  ongoing so so who knows what the outcome  of those will be - I haven't been  particularly impressed with the results  that we've seen so far we saw the  results from some of them from REM des  of year or trials the other day and the  ones in China really didn't go anywhere  the NIH ones that were done here in the  United States seem to maybe show some  progress but that's also an IV drug  which makes it just a lot more difficult  to get to a larger number of people so  if we find one of those that are already  approved for other infections or other  conditions then yes that I think that  those could be in place before we get to  a vaccine how long will it take to  develop herd immunity when you look at  the percent of the population that will  need to be infected by any kind of virus  to develop herd immunity that is based  on how transmissible they are in a  population you would have to have  basically about sixty percent sixty to  seventy percent of the population immune  in order to reach that herd immunity  threshold okay now remember herd  immunity is not like a switch it's not  all or nothing the you know the higher  number of people that you have in the  population that are immune the more  difficult it makes for that virus to  find a susceptible host and to spread so  you know any immunity in the population  is better than none even if it's not  that hard immunity threshold yet so  if we would go over the course of say  you know 12 months sorry and 40% of the  population would have been infected with  SARS coronavirus - in theory 40% of the  population then would be immune but what  if that immunity wanes after a year so  you have the people who were infected at  the very beginning have maybe you know  or maybe no longer functionally immune  so you're not really at 40% of the  population anymore you're at a lower  level than that so those are some of the  questions that we we just don't have  answers to and we won't have answers we  just can't have answers without  following people over time so it's only  something that we will know after you  know one year or three years how long  that immunity lasts and if people are  really protected from reinfection or not  a lot of health care workers are dying  from koban statistically speaking could  this be because of higher original  exposure that's one of the hypotheses  for the reasons why why some of these  health care workers are coming down with  not only illness but in some cases  really severe illness even though there  otherwise seems to be kind of young and  healthy is that exposure to a higher  load of virus and especially a higher  load of aerosolized virus they also did  see this with SARS especially in the  early days before again before it was  recognized and and before measures were  taken to to really use like n95  respirators and things like that to  protect healthcare workers that there  did seem to be kind of a dose exposure  there that's more virus you were exposed  to more severe symptoms you could get  and we have a little bit of evidence of  that from the early days in China also  again when health care workers were  exposed to this before understanding  what it was that's using again and 95  respirators and face masks and things  like that to protect healthcare workers  did seem to decrease their mortality as  an incidence of serious infection from  that as well so we think maybe again  that we that's something that will be  better resolved with good animal models  and animal experiments  where you can directly challenge them  with different doses of the virus and  look at the severity of their outcomes  so that's something that I'm sure will  be coming from some of these animal  studies please check out quantum  Magazine org and subscribe to the  YouTube channel where they have a lot of  other QAS with other scientists so thank  you for joining and stay safe out there  take care of each other thank you
 

